WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.920
Without Worlds, esports would look completely different.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:19.860
The stage shows you’re used to in every game from CS:GO to fighting games, the production

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.380
quality you hope to see in every esports stream, the level of play you want from the biggest

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.880
tournaments, all started at Worlds.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.360
Every year, Riot gathers the biggest teams in League of Legends to face off against one

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:34.900
another.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.200
The prize pool isn’t as big as some other events, but that hardly matters.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.260
Worlds is when everything is on the line.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:49.300
"The SKT Dynasty is over, all hail the new kings, Samsung Galaxy."

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.159
For the longest time, it was the only international tournament that mattered.

00:00:53.159 --> 00:00:59.629
But putting all that aside, Worlds changed esports, and every year, fans hope that Riot

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:01.370
can do it all over again.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.440
This is its story.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.960
These days, Worlds is one of the biggest productions

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:15.980
in esports.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:22.380
It's run as long as six weeks, takes place in massive venues all over the world, and dishes out

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.479
hundreds of thousands of dollars in prize money alongside one of esports’ most sought

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.079
after trophies, the Summoner’s Cup.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.960
But the first Worlds wasn’t the event that we’ve come to know today.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.520
"Now that we're wrapping up Season 1, we really want to continue to evolve the experience

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.480
to help establish it as one of the top esports titles in the world."

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.160
Worlds Season 1 was announced just two months before the tournament started, with teams

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.800
determined through a set of regional qualifiers.

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:54.180
Epik Gamer, Team Solomid and Counter Logic Gaming represented NA, Against All Authority,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:01.160
Fnatic and Gamed.de came from Europe, and Xan and Team Pacific qualified out of SEA.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:06.440
With the teams in place, the LoL community gathered at DreamHack Summer in Jonkoping,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:12.209
Sweden, in a venue that, somewhat lovingly, somewhat sarcastically, is referred to as

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:13.560
“Phreak’s Basement.”

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.420
"Ladies and gentlemen welcome back to the Elmia convention centre in Jönköping Sweden,

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.500
We are here for the Dreamhack Summer 2011 Championship Game,

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.380
The finals about to go underway, a hundred thousand dollar tournament help by League of Legends

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.400
and Riot Games.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.280
Obviously, DreamHack summer didn’t take place on the bottom floor of League caster

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.860
David “Phreak” Turley’s home, but the whole affair was pretty low key, especially

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.320
compared to its more recent iterations.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.240
Season 1 Worlds might not have featured the most exciting, high-level play by today’s

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.360
standards.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:50.000
(Casting)

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:51.500
But at the time, it was

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.640
the absolute peak of pro League of Legends.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.800
And it was only the beginning.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.480
"It's been an amazing end of our Season 1, and like Kevin said we're just getting started.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.600
Season 2 is going to be amazing."

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.560
"Absolutely, and thank you to everyone at home you've really made this event such an overwhelming success

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.560
we thank you so much for tuning in. And we'll see you next year."

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.600
Season 2 was the first Worlds that Riot had
planned in advance.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:20.840
Announced in May with regional finals taking place throughout the summer, Worlds’ structure

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:26.260
was more organized, the prize pool was higher and there were even more teams in attendance.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.300
It was the beginning of Worlds as we know it.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:34.900
"After the regional winners have been declared, the Top 12 teams will then put it all on the line

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.140
during the Season 2 World Championship in Los Angeles california."

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.000
And  all eyes were on European stalwarts, Moscow 5,

00:03:43.240 --> 00:04:04.020
(Casting)

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.440
And CLG Europe

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:16.880
(Casting)

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.600
But few anticipated the threats from the east.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.500
Azubu Frost and NaJin Black Sword dominated the group stage

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:36.100
(Casting)

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:40.920
While surprise winners Taipei Assassins came out of nowhere to take the top spot.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:56.200
(Casting)

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.199
And in the end, they hoisted the Summoner’s Cup.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:06.860
(Casting)

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:11.620
Of course, Riot makes video games, not trophies, and no one thought about the fact that the

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.220
winning team would actually have to lift it.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.880
Which led to the Summoner’s Cup weighing 70 pounds, the heaviest sports trophy in the world.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.940
Which some would argue is indicative of some of the growing pains that Riot had to deal

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:28.260
with at Season 2 Worlds in general.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.180
Season 2’s Worlds was bigger, and more impressive.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.820
The grand finals marked the first Western esports tournament to sell out a sports stadium.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.840
But Riot wasn’t as prepared for that as they’d hoped to be.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.080
The first, and perhaps most notable issue,
was the way the stage was set up.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.760
Players were seated in front of the screen, meaning they could just turn around and take a peak.

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.720
Most notably, Azubu Frost allegedly used this in a match against Team SoloMid to get a read

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.800
on a cheesy level 1 gank.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:19.800
(Casting)

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.400
But by the end of the tournament, Riot found four instances of screen cheating, even one

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.540
from TSM’s Dyrus who alleges he looked back to prove that players could see the screen.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:34.360
"And as soon as we saw that pause we were like "Holy sh*t our plan might not work." We looked straight

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.520
to Azubu Frost.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:39.700
And Chaox noticed their players looking at our map.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.440
Looking at the minimap, and they started talking.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:48.540
And then, there's also proof in the replay that there's been pings at dark areas where we're standing.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.820
Where they have no vision of.":

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:51.160
Riot let the players

00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:55.449
off with a fine, and admitted it was their mistake, but it marked one of the biggest

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:58.000
scandals in competitive League history.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:02.140
On top of that, viewers at home experienced some issues with the broadcast.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:08.200
The tournament was plagued by technical issues which meant plenty of pauses and plenty of Silver Scrapes.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:13.520
"So we are here at Day 4, they day that was never meant to happen, it is the extended world playoffs and as you

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.580
can see the construction is going on behind us right now, hopefully things will happen all to plan today."

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.780
But Riot took it all in stride.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.760
Worlds Season 2 was one of the most successful, widely viewed esports events the West had

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:32.640
ever seen, and Riot used it as a springboard to create one of the most important esports events in history.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.360
"Thank you for watching the Season 2 World Championships and we will see you in Season 3

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:38.960
goodnight!"

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.580
Before Worlds Season 3, the West’s esports scene was drastically different than what

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:52.760
it is today.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.700
Tournaments were small, often held in hotels or convention centers.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:04.160
Dota 2's the International was unique in 2013 for selling out a concert venue, but TI2 was tied to Pax Prime

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:05.680
and not its own event.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.320
Season 3 Worlds was the culmination of Riot’s efforts to bring as much of League of Legends

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.540
esports in house as possible.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:17.860
Instead of teams qualifying through regional qualifiers after a year of third party tournaments,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.280
they qualified through Riot’s own leagues.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:25.920
"The NA scene has gotten so strong, it's eight teams going in and competing for first place."

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.160
Riot set up the NA LCS and EU LCS, while licensing out league operations in China to

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:35.700
Tencent , Garena in South East Asia and OGN in Korea.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:40.100
Teams would qualify from around the world for a shot at the biggest tournament Western

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.880
esports had ever seen.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:47.000
Season 3 was held in California again, but this time, the Grand Finals took place at

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:52.260
the Staples Center, a 21,000 seat basketball and hockey stadium that had hosted the likes

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:57.310
of Kobe Bryant and Wayne Gretzky, making it the biggest audience to attend an esports

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:59.440
event in the West’s history.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:04.860
It was a landmark for the scene, and a sign that esports wasn’t just some passing fad.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:08.660
It was growing, and League of Legends was leading the charge.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:11.960
But it wasn’t just those optics that made people pay attention.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.200
Worlds Season 3 was all about the Korean Hype Train.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:24.720
"All aboard the Korean Hype Train SK Telecom T1."

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:29.760
After Korea’s strong performance the year before, all eyes were on NaJin Black Sword,

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:36.140
Samsung Ozone and of course, SK Telecom T1 and a young mid laner named Faker.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.620
But SKT weren’t the only strong team in attendance.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:44.880
Moscow 5’s legacy lived on in Gambit Gaming, who put on a strong performance in Group B

00:09:44.960 --> 00:10:13.760
(Casting)

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.720
Only trumped by Fnatic’s utter dominance in the same group

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:27.000
(Casting)

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:31.360
Meanwhile, Group A was ruled by SKT and China’s OMG

00:10:31.680 --> 00:11:00.240
(Casting)

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:06.480
And Chinese champions Royal Club, with superstar AD carry Uzi seemed unstoppable in the bracket,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.080
with wins over OMG

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:17.360
(Casting)

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:18.200
And Fnatic.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:34.480
(Casting)

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:39.600
But, as has become something of a Worlds tradition, one of the tournament’s best matches took

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:47.200
place in the semi finals, SKT and NaJin, two Korean titans, clashed for a spot in the Grand Finals.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:12:50.880
(Casting)

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:56.560
But it was SKT and Faker who struck the last blow, moving on to the finals, where they swept

00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:59.520
Royal to lift their first Summoner’s Cup.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:27.280
(Casting)

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:33.380
Season 3 Worlds was the beginning of a legacy that would go on to define competitive League of Legends.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:35.800
It  was the beginning of a saga that saw Faker

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:42.740
transform from a young prodigy into a champion and in turn the greatest player to ever touch the game.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:48.060
It was also yet another sign that North America, a strong region in the scene’s early days,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.700
was beginning to fall behind the competition.

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:56.020
TSM had attended all three Worlds up to that point, but bombed out of groups with a dismal

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:57.280
2-6 record.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:03.660
C9, the North American champions who dominated the NA LCS under the leadership Hai lost 2-1

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.720
to Fnatic in the quarterfinals.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:11.760
(Casting)

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.800
Despite the enormous hometown crowd, NA teams just couldn’t cut it at Worlds.

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:18.779
But the crowds didn’t care.

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:22.440
Once the NA teams were out, nationality was left at the door.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:27.000
Fans came to see the best League of Legends possible, and the teams delivered.

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:36.380
(Casting)

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:40.340
Between the storylines, the quality of the play, and of course, the incredible level

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:45.620
of production, there was nothing more impressive in esports that year than Worlds.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:51.240
Sure, ESL and DreamHack were running tournaments back then, but they didn’t look like they do now.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:56.340
TI was still a year away from ten million dollar prize pools and filling up Key Arena.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:01.680
Evo was years away from Mandalay Bay, and the CS:GO Majors hadn’t even started yet.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:03.400
There’s no question.

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:07.460
Worlds Season 3 set the standard for modern esports.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:11.240
Riot set the trend and showed the world what esports could look like.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.960
And most that came after, decided it was esports should look like.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:21.220
Every esports tournament wanted to fill up the Staples Center, but Worlds did it.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:26.860
"I really cannot believe I'm actually saying this, but that does it for the League of Legends Season 3

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:32.780
World Championship so on behalf of the entire broadcast team, goodnight and thanks for watching."

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:39.340
If Season 3 Worlds was Riot proving they could put on the biggest esports show in the West,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:44.260
Season 4 Worlds was them proving it could work on a global stage.

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:50.300
Season 4 took Worlds to East Asia, with matches played in Taipei, Singapore, Busan and Seoul.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.760
The finals were held in an even bigger venue than the Staples Center, the Seoul World Cup

00:15:54.760 --> 00:16:00.600
Stadium, a 66,000-seat arena that was built for the 2002 Fifa World Cup.

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.860
It was bigger in every way, with even more teams in attendance.

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:09.920
Riot opened up more spots for Southeast Asian and International Wildcard teams to compete,

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:15.480
which began a legacy of underdog teams playing spoiler and messing with the competition.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:20.440
KaBuM e-Sports may have finished last in their group, but their single win over Alliance

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:26.230
provided one of the most hype, least expected moments from the entire tournament, pushing

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:29.560
Alliance out of contention for a spot in the playoffs.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:35.140
"And I can't believe it Deman. Kabum takes the game over Alliance!"

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:39.540
"What the hell just happened!?"

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:46.520
But this time, all eyes were on Samsung White and Samsung Blue, the two best teams in Korea.

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:50.380
Both teams were strong, but since Blue had defeated White throughout the year, many saw

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:52.080
them as a slight favourite.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:56.280
But after a shocking 3-0 victory for White
in the semifinals,

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:08.660
(Casting)

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:11.480
Blue’s captain, Dade, the general, handed

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:16.100
his signature jacket over to PawN to signify a changing of the guard.

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:20.720
And with that jacket on his back, PawN made sure to leave his mark.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:34.640
(Casting)

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:39.020
But Season 4 Worlds wasn’t the biggest esports event of 2014.

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:43.320
After the incredible show Riot had put on the year before, several esports tournament

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:45.520
organizers stepped up their game.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:50.840
Most notably, the Dota 2 community put together the largest prize pool in esports history

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:57.700
for TI4, with $10.9 million shared across the 14 participating teams.

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:01.300
Riot didn’t increase their prize pool for Worlds in response.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:06.640
In fact, it stayed at just over 2 million, taking some of the wind out of their sails.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:11.780
Sure, Riot took the tournament on a grand tour of Europe, from London to Paris to Berlin,

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:15.720
but it somehow didn’t feel as big as some of the competition.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:20.380
On top of that , the tournament ran in the middle of one of Riot’s most frustrating metas.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:26.480
The juggernaut meta defined worlds 2015, with the same champions being picked and banned

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:28.120
for most of the playoffs.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:34.640
"And as for EDG's bans I think since they're on red side. They may just be locked into the gank plank

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:36.160
Mordekaiser and Elise."

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.160
Partway into the tournament, Gragas, Ziggs and Lux were disabled as well, bringing champion

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.400
diversity even further down.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:48.620
Gragas was one of just a few viable junglers in the meta, and without him, fans got very

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:52.880
used to watching the same comps duke it out over the course of a month.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:58.000
"The issue that appeared in game prevented Reignover from casting the Gragas Q spell and the

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:05.200
refereeing team they did investigate and exhaust every single option to resolve the issue in-game.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.880
Including allowing Gragas to die and attempt to resolve the matter."

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:13.760
As for the games themselves, Samsung’s win the previous season highlighted the Korean

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:19.840
exodus, which saw a number of LPL teams sign top-tier LCK players in the hopes of finally

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:26.100
breaking China’s second-place curse at Worlds. This included all members of both Samsung teams.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:31.380
It wasn’t the first time a defending champion wasn’t in attendance to defend their championship,

00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:35.940
but after two straight years of Korean dominance, many hoped that the streak would break.

00:19:36.160 --> 00:19:41.960
Cloud9, who ran the impossible gauntlet to make it to Worlds 2015 looked incredible,

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.620
going undefeated in their first week of groups.

00:19:44.720 --> 00:20:11.820
(Casting)

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:17.600
But couldn’t keep the momentum going, and along with the rest of NA, went winless in week two.

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:23.540
"What a heartbreaking defeat though, Cloud9 getting essentially reverse swept here 3-0."

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:30.580
Meanwhile, following TSM’s disappointing finish, beloved top laner Marcus “Dyrus”

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:34.080
Hill announced his retirement during  the tournament, bringing the crowd to tears,

00:20:34.080 --> 00:20:36.980
providing one of League’s most touching moments.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:43.660
"Now it's time to open a new book, right now my story ends here but I still

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:51.440
even with me gone I still want everyone to support TSM and all of my teammates."

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.700
"TSM! TSM! TSM!"

00:20:55.700 --> 00:21:01.590
Not only did Korea continue to dominate, the grand finalists, Koo Tigers and SKT, swept

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:04.320
nearly every team they played.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:09.360
The only team able to put up a fight against either was KT Rolster, the third Korean team

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:10.360
at the tournament.

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:17.400
And in those Grand Finals, Faker led SKT to victory once again, making the team the first ever two time

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:18.600
World Champions.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:35.320
(Casting)

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:40.660
All eyes are on League of Legends during Worlds.

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:46.340
The venues wow the crowds and the champions display the absolute highest level of play.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:51.180
But between the prize pool discrepancy, North America’s complete disaster of a tournament,

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:56.140
and the terrible meta, Riot needed to ratchet things up for Season 6.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.020
Season 6 Worlds did bring some changes.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:03.480
For one, Riot actually increased the prize pool through fan contributions.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:08.740
And while it wasn’t a scratch on TI’s prize pool, the increase to $5 million was

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:09.960
a welcome change

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:15.580
Then, perhaps in response to the sudden meta change ahead of Worlds 2015, Riot played it

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:21.400
on a patch nearly a month old, giving players plenty of time to experiment with new picks,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.400
like Gorilla’s Miss Fortune support.

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:25.060
"An insta lock Miss Fortune."

00:22:25.060 --> 00:22:27.600
"Something really weird I don't care about my last point."

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:33.220
And to bring it all together, Riot took worlds back to NA, back to the Staples Center.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:36.180
Things weren’t perfect, but the tournament was more exciting.

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:42.180
Europe seemed stronger, with H2K taking first in their group in a tiebreaker match against EDG.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:49.380
(Casting)

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:54.340
Wildcard legends Albus Nox Luna made a deep tournament run, becoming the first wildcard

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:56.080
team to make it out of groups,

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:02.420
"All I want to say is that, hey guys, we are from wildcard and they have told you before,

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:06.060
being underdog doesn't mean being a loser, that's all."

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.920
And Samsung Galaxy provided the first ever five

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:12.960
game Worlds Grand Finals against defending champions SKT.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:24:08.500
(Casting)

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:12.720
But of course, SKT prevailed, turning their legacy into a dynasty.

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:17.600
With three world championship titles under their belts, it seems impossible for anyone

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:23.860
to ever upset their place as League’s greatest team and Faker as League’s greatest player.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.140
But every dynasty has to fall eventually.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:35.260
Worlds 2017 took things to China for the first time, with a tour through Wuhan, Guangzhou,

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:40.140
Shanghai and Beijing, ending the tournament in the Bird’s Nest, the biggest venue in

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:41.720
League of Legends history.

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:55.940
This time, Riot shook things up with a larger play-in stage, that saw the lowest seeds from

00:24:55.960 --> 00:25:02.720
every region but Korea play against the champions of the Wildcard regions for four slots in the group stage.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:09.440
Gigabyte marines provided 2017's fun wildcard matches, taking games off Immortals and Fnatic.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:14.160
And while they didn't make it out of groups, neither did two of the North American teams.

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:19.680
But Cloud9, ever the Worlds hopefuls, made it to the quarterfinals, and took Team WE

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:25.000
to five games, giving NA fans just a little more faith in their region.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:35.240
(Casting)

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:40.480
Meanwhile, all eyes were on Longzhu Gaming, the first seed out of Korea and new favourites

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:42.320
to take SKT’s crown.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:47.000
Unfortunately, they fell to Samsung Galaxy, 3-0, in the quarterfinals.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:53.600
(Casting)

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:59.680
While both Misfits and Royal Never Give Up both pushed SKT to five games, back-to-back.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:15.960
(Casting)

00:26:15.960 --> 00:26:21.520
In the end, Samsung emerged victorious, with a swift 3-0 victory over SKT, earning

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:26.220
their org a second Worlds trophy and ending the SKT dynasty.

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:33.260
"The SKT dynasty is over, all hail the new kings, Samsung Galaxy!"

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:37.060
"Your 2017 World Champions."

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:43.160
It shocked the world, but perhaps more importantly, it shocked Faker, who was moved to tears by

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.840
his first major international loss in years.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:52.020
Worlds isn’t the only tournament that matters anymore.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:57.700
For League, there’s MSI to show off international strength, and as for esports in general, tournaments

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:03.700
like TI, the Majors, even Evo have stepped up their production values to match Worlds.

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:08.220
But somehow, Worlds is still magical every time.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:12.640
It’s not just about the best teams in the world competing on the world stage.

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:17.420
It’s not just about the tours Riot makes, taking fans from venue to venue with a unique

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:18.540
show in each one.

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:24.960
It’s not just about the extraordinary levels of production that Riot somehow manages to top each year.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:28.040
It’s about what the tournament stands for.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:30.420
Worlds pushed esports forwards.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:33.660
It cemented what the modern major tournament should look like.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.410
What it should feel like.

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:37.500
For both the players and the fans.

00:27:37.500 --> 00:27:42.640
Ever since that grand finals at the Staples Center, esports has never turned back.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:49.040
Worlds changed esports, and every year, fans tune in to see if they can do it again.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:01.520
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

